Here is the single, self-contained setup.yaml file containing all Kubernetes manifests (PostgreSQL ConfigMap, Deployment, ClusterIP Service, and the Pandas Pod), along with the complete execution script to run it from scratch.
1. Complete Unified Kubernetes Manifest (setup.yaml)
# 1. ConfigMap: Inserts sample table and data into PostgreSQL on startup
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: postgres-init-script
data:
  init.sql: |
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (
        id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
        name VARCHAR(50),
        role VARCHAR(50)
    );
    INSERT INTO users (name, role) VALUES ('Alice', 'DevOps Lead'), ('Bob', 'Data Analyst');
---
# 2. Deployment: Runs PostgreSQL image
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: postgres
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: postgres
        image: postgres:15-alpine
        env:
        - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
          value: "postgres"
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5432
        volumeMounts:
        - name: init-volume
          mountPath: /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d
      volumes:
      - name: init-volume
        configMap:
          name: postgres-init-script
---
# 3. Service: Exposes PostgreSQL internally on ClusterIP (postgres-service:5432)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres-service
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  ports:
  - port: 5432
    targetPort: 5432
  selector:
    app: postgres
---
# 4. Pod: Runs Python Pandas, connects to ClusterIP, reads SQL table, prints df.loc[0]
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: pandas-app
  labels:
    app: pandas-app
spec:
  containers:
  - name: pandas-container
    image: python:3.10-slim
    command: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
    args:
    - |
      pip install --no-cache-dir pandas psycopg2-binary sqlalchemy && \
      python3 -c "
      import pandas as pd
      from sqlalchemy import create_engine

      db_url = 'postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-service:5432/postgres'
      engine = create_engine(db_url)

      # Query table into Pandas DataFrame
      df = pd.read_sql('SELECT * FROM users;', engine)

      print('=== Full DataFrame ===')
      print(df)

      print('\n=== Executed df.loc[0] ===')
      print(df.loc[0])
      " && \
      sleep 3600

2. Run Commands & Check Logs
Execute the following terminal commands to deploy the resources and check the Pandas output:
# 1. Apply all Kubernetes resources
kubectl apply -f setup.yaml

# 2. Wait for the pandas container to finish installation and execution
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/pandas-app --timeout=60s

# 3. View the container logs (shows df.loc[0])
kubectl logs pandas-app

# 4. Execute df.loc[0] directly inside the container via kubectl exec
kubectl exec -it pandas-app -- python3 -c "
import pandas as pd
from sqlalchemy import create_engine
engine = create_engine('postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-service:5432/postgres')
df = pd.read_sql('SELECT * FROM users;', engine)
print('Direct Exec Output:')
print(df.loc[0])
"